g true to the book in terms of the  events it makes it feel different and  very fresh and of course for me that I'm  saying that relative is the nineties  movie but again speaking a little bit  for my partner Liz affirm the same thing  as someone who knows the book really  well it makes it feel it's a really neat  kind of magic trick because it makes it  feel fresh and new without actually  changing the substance of like any of it  that's a neat trick and I wouldn't I  feel like a lot of this was done to  create sort of thematic rhyming of  scenes to have to be able to complement  and show very close together if not  back-to-back scenes of events when they  are younger that and then some  complementary scenes from when they're  older that relate in some way to show  how things have stayed the same how  things have changed  and to pair those things off and I've  heard some people concerned that it was  confusing and if someone did find it  confusing I'm not gonna say you're wrong  but I didn't find it confusing I thought  it was brilliant I thought it was  amazing the the way that the temporal  trickery of it and to have it flow as  naturally and make again for me at least  absolute sense at every step of the way  man that's a trick to pull and Amy is a  character who I think benefits a lot  from that because in the book if you  don't know the story Amy is kind of  awful as a little kid she is spoiled she  she engages in temper tantrums and she  does some really hurtful stuff um when  she's young so when you read the book or  when you see any other adaptation that's  just going straight chronological what  that means is you see her at her worst  early on and for quite a chunk of time  and then you're expected to kind of  sympathize with her later and it's not  impossible but it's tricky whereas with  this you actually get to know her better  as I kind of pulled together adults  and then you learn about her backstory  and go oh she was just kind of a little  demon whe